      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. My concern isn't paperwork. A 50 mg daily dose of amlodipine is five times the maximum and could seriously harm or kill the patient. Confirming it doesn't make the dose safer, and the order still needs to come from the patient's prescriber.\n\nIf the patient needs amlodipine, here's the quickest way to get it done:\n1. **Contact the treating physician or the medicine team's on-call prescriber.** They can decide the right dose. The usual starting dose is 5 mg once daily, and the maximum is 10 mg.\n2. **Have them place or confirm the order.** I can enter it as soon as they give me a dose in the safe range.\n3. **If there's an urgent clinical issue**, such as very high blood pressure, the bedside nurse or a rapid response team can get a clinician there right away.\n\nIf you meant to write 5 mg, have the prescriber confirm that, and I'll enter it right away."
      }
